# Day 40

**40km (on bike) + 3km (on foot) / 6.5 hours**

*Ngaherenga campsite to Piropiro campsite*

Cycling! That's the way to spice things up a bit after the monotony of yesterday. The Timber Trail is the next section and it's commonly cycled rather than walked so it makes sense to take the cycling option. Here's the pros and cons of it from my perspective:

*Pros:*

- Different, a sense of variety
- Exciting, much more active
- Quick, can go much faster than otherwise. Saves a day or more and means I don't have to do massively long days
- Travel with a smaller day pack 
- Don't have to get overtaken by cyclists and dodge collisions constantly 
- Reduces pressure on tired muscles and body parts so they get some of a rest

*Cons:*

- Costs money to hire a mountain bike
- Dangerous, can hurt yourself, perhaps severely
- Don't get to take in the sights as much - going quickly and can't take out phone for photos
- Hurts muscles that won't be used to the cycling
- Makes the trip impure in that it's not all walking on land - this might sound silly but actually was part of my consideration 

In the end I decided to go for it and am mostly glad I did. It was absolutely frosty in the morning but we had time to wait before the bike company showed up anyway so was manageable. I haven't biked in a long time so was apprehensive about the whole thing and when we got started with some practice around the carpark I managed to immediately fall over. Great job me. I only picked up a few scrapes but my ego was well checked from it.

After we got going properly I had another problem in that I was really struggling to go uphill. I have cycled a decent amount previously but it was a long time ago and that was mostly road cycling whereas this was mountain biking. The track was well maintained but it began with a steady incline and I was unable to sustain that for long. Even on a low gear my leg muscles would really struggle and I would have to get off and do the walk of shame with my bike. 

![Me walking my bike up a hilly section](./cycling.avif)

I had to do this an embarrassing amount. Some of the others had ebikes which gave them a boost and made those sections so much more comfortable but I was stuck with just my legs and they were not strong enough to manage. An advantage of the bikes is that you can pass the kilometres so much quicker. When walking each kilometre seems to take an age but on a bike it's no time at all when the terrain is comfortable. 

After 10km or so there was a walking track side track which seemed like a great option to switch things up and see the summit. It was only a short walk and felt breezy with only day packs on even though it was uphill. The view from the top was pretty good although the clouds did obscure the mountains in the distance and prevent a stunning panoramic view. Had lunch up on the top viewing Lake Taupo in the distance.

![The view from the summit](./summit.avif)

After that small optional trek and making our way back down to the bikes we continued. After just a little more incline the track became way nicer, being mostly flat with just a few up and down sections which is much more doable for me, requiring much fewer walks of shame. However it wasn't to last as the track became tricky in the other direction being too steep and windy downwards. This required heavy use of the brakes and I wasn't comfortable keeping it at a steady pace while also turning around sharp corners so I managed another fall off the bike when I slowed down too much. Again some more scrapes. Ugh.

After another 13km or so there was a magnificent suspension bridge, wide enough for a bike and strong enough not to wobble too much. Pretty cool. Unfortunately I am not good at cycling straight when trying to glance off the bridge so I managed to bump the sides but was alright. After that there was more up and down, more bridges, some roads and even some steep descent that required strong use of the brakes to keep the speed in check and felt nerve-wracking to me. There was also some really bumpy sections that would probably be more comfortable if you were travelling faster but I wasn't comfortable doing that so I just had to suffer the bumps of the tree roots or track. 

In the end the distance flew by as it was so much faster on a bike and the day seemed to end super quickly. For the last 5km I tried to make sure not to walk the bike at all which was really tough on the legs but I managed, mostly by keeping momentum into the uphill climbs and powering through them. There's two days of cycling so one more ahead which is exciting.